WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.660
Who in this room is personally
accountable if we are hit?

00:00:03.945 --> 00:00:07.975
Two European rules,
NIS2 and DORA, now name the board.

00:00:09.383 --> 00:00:11.113
First, the board is accountable.

00:00:11.573 --> 00:00:17.013
NIS2 requires the management body to
approve the cybersecurity risk measures

00:00:17.013 --> 00:00:18.583
and oversee them.

00:00:18.763 --> 00:00:20.973
It can be held liable when it does not.

00:00:21.243 --> 00:00:22.013
In the Netherlands,

00:00:22.403 --> 00:00:26.753
the Cbw has been in force
since 15 August 2026.

00:00:27.163 --> 00:00:28.813
DORA has applied to banks,

00:00:29.463 --> 00:00:33.753
insurers and investment
firms since 17 January 2025.

00:00:34.183 --> 00:00:35.913
Delegating the work is allowed.

00:00:36.278 --> 00:00:38.608
Delegating the responsibility is not.

00:00:39.717 --> 00:00:41.907
Second, reporting runs on a clock.

00:00:42.227 --> 00:00:47.517
NIS2 requires an early warning
within 24 hours of becoming aware.

00:00:47.792 --> 00:00:51.452
An incident notification
follows within 72 hours,

00:00:51.452 --> 00:00:54.182
and a final report within one month.

00:00:54.537 --> 00:00:59.897
DORA asks financial entities for an
initial notification within 4 hours of

00:00:59.897 --> 00:01:02.507
classifying an incident as major.

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:06.520
Third, your suppliers'
security is your duty.

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:10.570
You are expected to know who they are,
and to assess their risk.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:12.570
Put obligations in the contract.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.570
Under DORA, have an exit plan
for the ones you cannot lose.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:18.810
Blaming the supplier is an explanation,

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:20.350
not an excuse.

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:23.440
Fourth, the rules ask for proof.

00:01:23.745 --> 00:01:26.015
A policy document is not evidence.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:31.150
NIS2 requires board members to
follow cybersecurity training.

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:33.150
Testing shows the measures work.

00:01:33.415 --> 00:01:34.705
Does the backup restore?

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:36.930
Does the incident process hold?

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:40.890
Financial entities face the
strongest version under DORA.

00:01:41.917 --> 00:01:43.127
What good looks like.

00:01:43.412 --> 00:01:46.402
One named executive,
accountable to the board.

00:01:46.697 --> 00:01:49.567
A written, dated,
board-approved risk framework.

00:01:50.227 --> 00:01:51.817
A reporting drill against the clock.

00:01:52.472 --> 00:01:55.842
A supplier register,
with contract clauses and exit plans.

00:01:56.242 --> 00:02:00.512
And evidence: minutes, approvals,
test reports and training records.

00:02:02.363 --> 00:02:03.573
A suggested first step.

00:02:03.858 --> 00:02:05.928
Ask who in this room owns it, by name.

00:02:06.178 --> 00:02:09.568
Then ask when the board last saw evidence,
not assurances.

00:02:10.118 --> 00:02:12.528
Have legal confirm which regime applies.

00:02:14.217 --> 00:02:16.547
Compliance is the floor, not the goal.

00:02:16.837 --> 00:02:19.047
Name the owner, and ask for evidence.

